course: dictionary
lesson: 15064
title: Many meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and revival is one of them. The main idea is coming back: back to life, back to health, or back into fashion. «The old cinema showed a revival of a famous film from the 1950s.» The film was not new. It came back so people could see it again.
explain: Use the context to choose the right meaning. «After the drought, the rain brought a revival of the crops.» The crops were weak and dry, then the rain made them strong again, so revival means getting back strength. Ask yourself which meaning fits the clues.
explain: Nearby words are clues too. «The town held a revival meeting» tells you it is a church meeting, where people turn to faith with new energy. «There is a revival of interest in vinyl records» tells you people are keen on something again after it had been less popular.
fact: Revival is a noun. Its plural is revivals.
fact: «Revival» can mean coming back to life, health, strength or popularity.
fact: In a theatre, «revival» means a new performance of an old play or show.
fact: In church, «revival» can mean a time when many people turn to faith with new energy, or a lively religious meeting.
fact: «Revival» can also mean bringing someone back to consciousness, such as after a faint.
exercise: «The old cinema showed a revival of a famous film from the 1950s.» What is a revival here? | A new film that has never been shown before | A film made in the 1950s and then thrown away | *A new showing of an old film | A cinema that has closed for good
exercise: «After the drought, the rain brought a revival of the crops.» What does revival mean here? | *Coming back to strength and health | Being sold at the market | Being cut down at harvest time | Losing their colour in the sun
exercise: «The town held a revival meeting on Sunday.» What kind of meeting is this? | A meeting about new roads | *A lively religious meeting | A quiet meeting for a book club | A school sports day
test: «The nurse's care brought about a revival of the boy who had fainted.» What does revival mean here? | Moving him to a new house | Making a loud noise | Sending him home to sleep | *Bringing him back to consciousness
test: Is revival a noun, and which word is its plural? | revivaled | revivial | revives | *revivals
exam: «There is a revival of interest in vinyl records.» What does revival mean here? | *People becoming keen on something again after it had been less popular | Vinyl records being thrown away | A loud noise from a speaker | A record shop closing down
